<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexandre Dumas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1802">1802</a>–<a href="/years/1870">1870</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Alexandre Dumas père, Alexandre Davy de la Pailleterie</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexandre Dumas, also known as Alexandre Dumas père, was a French novelist and playwright born in Villers-Cotterêts. His father, General Thomas-Alexandre Dumas, was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> to a French nobleman and Marie-Cessette Dumas, an enslaved woman. Dumas began his career writing plays and became one of the most widely read French authors with historical novels of adventure first published as serials, including The Count of Monte Cristo and The Three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">Musketeers</a>. In the 1840s he founded the Théâtre Historique in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. He died on 5 December 1870 and was buried at his birthplace.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexandre-dumas.jpg" alt="Alexandre Dumas" width="330" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nadar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nadar_-_Alexander_Dumas_père_(1802-1870)_-_Google_Art_Project_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alexandre Dumas was born Alexandre Davy de la Pailleterie in 1802 at Villers-Cotterêts, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisne">Aisne</a>, the son of Marie-Louise Élisabeth Labouret, an innkeeper's daughter, and Thomas-Alexandre Dumas. His father was the natural son of the Marquis Alexandre Antoine Davy de la Pailleterie, a French nobleman and artillery officer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>, and Marie-Cessette Dumas, an enslaved woman whom the marquis had bought with her daughter and made his concubine.</p><p>In 1775 the marquis left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to claim the family estates and title. Shortly before leaving he sold Marie-Cessette and her daughters, but he kept Thomas-Alexandre, then 14, and took him to France, where the boy was freed and educated at a military school. He was promoted to general by the age of 31, the first soldier of Afro-Antilles origin to reach that rank in the French army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>While working for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i">Louis-Philippe</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, Dumas began to write magazine articles and plays. His first play, Henry III and His Court, was produced in 1829, when he was 27, and was acclaimed; Christine followed the next year with equal success, and the income allowed him to write full-time. In 1830 he took part in the revolution that replaced Charles X with his former employer.</p><p>He then turned to novels, publishing them as serials in the newspapers. The first was La Comtesse de Salisbury in 1836, and in 1838 he rewrote one of his plays as the serial Le Capitaine Paul. After the election of Louis-Napoléon Bonaparte in 1851 he fell from favour and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In 1861 he founded the newspaper L'Indépendant, which supported Italian unification, and he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1864.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Dumas married the actress Ida Ferrier on 1 February 1840; the scholar Claude Schopp lists nearly 40 mistresses, and he fathered at least four children, among them the novelist and playwright Alexandre Dumas, fils. He died at the age of 68 of natural causes, possibly a heart attack, and his death was overshadowed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>.</p><p>The Paris Metro named a station after him in 1970, and his country house outside Paris, the Château de Monte-Cristo, has been restored as a museum. For the bicentenary of his birth in 2002, President Jacques Chirac had his remains transferred to the Panthéon, over the initial opposition of villagers in Villers-Cotterêts, who cited his wish to be buried there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alexandre Dumas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In France, Chateaubriand, Lamartine, Hugo, <b>Alexandre Dumas</b> and George Sand led in literature, and Delacroix and Berlioz in painting and music.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The word began as a term of disapproval: “féminisme” first appeared in an 1871 French medical thesis on men with tuberculosis who had developed feminine traits, and in 1872 <b>Alexandre Dumas</b> fils coined “féministe” for men who supported women’s rights.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/feminism">Feminism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The book had a major influence on the literary community and is directly referenced in 19th-century French works such as <b>Alexandre Dumas</b>'s The Three Musketeers and Edmond Rostand's Cyrano de Bergerac.</blockquote><a href="/work/don-quixote">Don Quixote · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
